Issue: The baseline (f0b6817, average 0.4311) addressed every Train item through a retrieval tool that never exposed the examples' own answer strings as the answer space, so the solver invented its own wording: 23 of 96 successful LawBench executions produced the right charge with a trailing "罪" that the Train targets never carry (e.g. `[罪名]单位行贿罪<eoa>` vs the gold `单位行贿`), only 1/96 predictions was character-identical to any Train target, and single-part answers were given for the 26/46 Val questions whose gold label has several ";"-separated parts. Symptom2Disease showed the same drift (0.84), and 13/60 USPTO executions never called the retrieval tool at all.

Modification: Made the retrieval step ground the answer string instead of only the question. `solver/tools/retrieval.py` now separates example formatting from a new `format_answer_key`, which deduplicates the frozen Train answers, orders them most frequent first, and appends them to the tool output under a 4000-char budget as an "answer key" with copy-verbatim rules (no added characters, category words, titles or punctuation; multi-part answers use the key's separator and keep every part). `solver/skills/answer-with-examples/SKILL.md` now makes that key and the examples the authority on answer wording and requires character-for-character copying, including when an answer is not listed (same style). No dataset names, label lists or dataset-identity branching were introduced; the key is derived at run time from the frozen Train memory only.

